<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cacheu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cacheu is a town on the Cacheu river in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a>, founded by the Portuguese in 1588 as a fortified trading post. It was one of the oldest European settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, dominated by Luso-African traders. Its small 16th-century fort still stands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cacheu.jpg" alt="Cacheu" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jcornelius · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Forte_de_Cacheu_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cacheu lies on the Cacheu River in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a> and is the capital of the region of the same name. It stands in the territory of the Papel people, and its name is of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bainuk">Bainuk</a> origin, from Caticheu, meaning the place where we rest. Its strategic position on the river made it one of the earliest European colonial settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the late sixteenth century a European and Afro-European population grew up through informal settlement by Portuguese traders, adventurers and outcasts known as lançados. Portugal also sent degredados, people condemned to exile. In 1567 the English slaver John Hawkins raided the settlement. After this and a period of conflict with the Papel, the traders asked the king of Cacheu in 1589 for permission to build a fort and in 1591 moved into the new stockade. The Papel stormed it without success before an agreement allowed both to live together. A priest was appointed in 1598, and in 1605 the crown offered a municipal charter, when about 1,500 people lived there, 500 of them white.</p><p>Cacheu was an important slave-trading point for the Portuguese in Upper Guinea and a centre of boat-building, with mostly African artisans. The lançados, Papel and other traders regularly broke the crown's supposed monopoly. In 1676 the Portuguese launched the Company of Cacheu and Rivers and Commerce of Guinea, the first of several companies that failed to control the trade. In 1684 the slave trader Bibiana Vaz captured the captain-major, and the so-called Republic of Cacheu refused to let the company's representatives land. In 1878 a Portuguese force attacked Cacanda, the first of the campaigns that ended in the conquest of the region in 1914. As Portuguese Guinea grew, Bolama, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bissau">Bissau</a> and Canchungo overtook Cacheu as administrative centres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A stone fort built in the 1640s still stands. The Cacheu Memorial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> and Black Traffic, a museum, gives historical context to the area, and the Cacheu River Mangroves Natural Park lies nearby. A regular market serves the surrounding districts, which export coconuts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Cacheu is twinned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cacheu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade ran through coastal intermediaries and Luso-African communities at ports such as <b>Cacheu</b>, founded by the Portuguese in 1588, and later through British and French traders on the Gambia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1588 the town of <b>Cacheu</b> was founded as a fortified trading base, followed by Bissau in the late 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-guinea">Portuguese Guinea</a></li></ul></section>
